{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"complete\":true,\"findings\":[{\"rule_id\":\"R1\",\"file_id\":\"r1/dispatch.py.txt\",\"start_line\":329,\"end_line\":329,\"quote\":\"    customer_id = required(record, \\\"customer_id\\\")\",\"explanation\":\"The raw delivery adapter does not validate `customer_id` as a nonempty string before placing it in the trusted `Delivery` record. The module contract says identifiers are nonempty strings, and this identifier is retained for ownership checks.\",\"consequence\":\"Malformed or missing customer ownership data can enter the trusted record and undermine later ownership checks.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R1\",\"file_id\":\"r1/dispatch.py.txt\",\"start_line\":398,\"end_line\":398,\"quote\":\"    if amount > declared:\",\"explanation\":\"The claim adapter does not enforce the documented invariant that the claim is opened before its deadline; it accepts any `opened_at` and `deadline` values that pass the nonnegative-integer checks.\",\"consequence\":\"A claim outside its allowed filing window can be accepted as a valid trusted record.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R1\",\"file_id\":\"r1/dispatch.py.txt\",\"start_line\":296,\"end_line\":296,\"quote\":\"    if origin == destination:\",\"explanation\":\"The route adapter checks that endpoints differ but never checks that `arrive_at` follows `depart_at`, despite the function contract describing a route in time order.\",\"consequence\":\"A route whose arrival precedes or equals departure can enter the trusted route record and produce invalid scheduling results.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R3\",\"file_id\":\"r3/planning.py.txt\",\"start_line\":12,\"end_line\":12,\"quote\":\"from time import time\",\"explanation\":\"The planning module imports a clock source for a pure typed decision; `urgency` uses it instead of the explicit `now` argument.\",\"consequence\":\"The same validated inputs can yield different urgency results at different times, and callers cannot reliably control the observation time.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R3\",\"file_id\":\"r3/planning.py.txt\",\"start_line\":176,\"end_line\":176,\"quote\":\"    remaining = order.due_at - int(time()) - travel\",\"explanation\":\"`urgency` ignores its `now` parameter and reads the system clock, violating the requirement that a pure decision depend only on its explicit inputs.\",\"consequence\":\"Urgency results can be wrong relative to the orchestrator's supplied observation time.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R3\",\"file_id\":\"r3/planning.py.txt\",\"start_line\":203,\"end_line\":203,\"quote\":\"    orders.sort(key=lambda order: (-order.priority, order.due_at, order.order_id))\",\"explanation\":\"`load_sequence` sorts the caller's input list in place, mutating an argument in a decision described as deterministic and input-preserving.\",\"consequence\":\"Other callers observing the same list see a changed order after the decision runs.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R3\",\"file_id\":\"r3/planning.py.txt\",\"start_line\":225,\"end_line\":225,\"quote\":\"        leg = legs.pop(0)\",\"explanation\":\"`next_stops` removes legs from the caller's list while computing a result, contrary to the pure-decision rule against mutating arguments.\",\"consequence\":\"The route input is depleted and cannot be safely reused by subsequent decisions.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R3\",\"file_id\":\"r3/planning.py.txt\",\"start_line\":263,\"end_line\":263,\"quote\":\"                alternatives.remove(leg)\",\"explanation\":\"`reschedule` removes an alternative from the caller's list while evaluating it, mutating its input.\",\"consequence\":\"Subsequent decisions receive a changed set of alternatives and may produce different results.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R4\",\"file_id\":\"r4/ledger.py.txt\",\"start_line\":78,\"end_line\":78,\"quote\":\"        raise ReadError(\\\"cannot save event\\\") from exc\",\"explanation\":\"The event insert helper translates an integrity failure into `ReadError`, although its docstring declares `WriteError` for insert failures. Callers such as `allocate` therefore do not receive the documented write-failure type.\",\"consequence\":\"Callers handling `WriteError` can miss an event-write failure, despite the transaction rolling back.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R4\",\"file_id\":\"r4/ledger.py.txt\",\"start_line\":149,\"end_line\":149,\"quote\":\"            connection = sqlite3.connect(self.database)\",\"explanation\":\"`payment` acquires a database connection without a closing context manager and does not close it after fetching the row, including when the row is absent.\",\"consequence\":\"Repeated calls can leave SQLite connections and their resources open.\",\"severity\":\"major\"},{\"rule_id\":\"R4\",\"file_id\":\"r4/ledger.py.txt\",\"start_line\":300,\"end_line\":300,\"quote\":\"        except sqlite3.Error:\\n            return False\",\"explanation\":\"`set_state` suppresses database and commit failures and returns `False`, although its documented contract requires a `WriteError` for update or commit failure.\",\"consequence\":\"Callers cannot distinguish a failed write from an update that matched no booking; the required error is lost.\",\"severity\":\"major\"}]}"}}
